  "findings": [
    "61 of 62 original Rust source files are byte-identical; only login/dpop.rs changes, plus three new private/test files.",
    "All published feature definitions and rand 0.8.5 dependency configuration match exactly; p256 0.13.2 to 0.14.0 is the only production dependency change.",
    "SigningRng forwards next_u32, next_u64 and fill_bytes directly to the same OS-seeded StdRng; no entropy substitution, buffering, truncation or seed reuse was introduced. Infallible correctly enables rand_core 0.10 blanket Rng/CryptoRng implementations.",
    "DPoP remains SEC1 input, uncompressed SEC1 public coordinates, ES256 and 64-byte r||s URL-safe unpadded signature encoding. New tests verify with the upgraded p256 and independently with ring when sso is enabled.",
    "Provider tests call actual LoginCredentialsProvider, observe actual outbound signed refresh requests, preserve dpopKey/clientId, instantiate a new provider from the cache, reject expired credentials inside the throttle window and retain cache bytes on a 403 response.",
    "Provider tests inject an in-memory Fs and recording HTTP connector: these prove real provider/cache/serialization behavior, not real AWS network authentication or an OS-process/disk restart.",
    "test-003 log reports 19 login tests passed. test-006 reports 242 of 244 run tests passed, two failures and one pre-existing skip. The failures contain HTTP 503 responses with server envoy to endpoints expected to time out; the all-provider gate remains failed, not waived."
